import{_ as t,o,c as a,a2 as r}from"./chunks/framework.CKcXoiDW.js";const m=JSON.parse('{"title":"Workouts have a format, not a type","description":"","frontmatter":{"status":"accepted","date":"2026-10-26T00:00:00.000Z"},"headers":[],"relativePath":"docs/adr/0013-workouts-have-a-format-not-a-type.md","filePath":"docs/adr/0013-workouts-have-a-format-not-a-type.md"}'),s={name:"docs/adr/0013-workouts-have-a-format-not-a-type.md"};function n(i,e,h,d,c,l){return o(),a("div",null,[...e[0]||(e[0]=[r('<h1 id="workouts-have-a-format-not-a-type" tabindex="-1">Workouts have a format, not a type <a class="header-anchor" href="#workouts-have-a-format-not-a-type" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Workouts have a format, not a type&quot;">​</a></h1><p>Supersedes <a href="./0004-workout-types-are-modules-over-one-engine">ADR 0004</a>.</p><p>A workout no longer has a workout type (strength, conditioning, rehab, mobility, treadmill). It has a <strong>format</strong>, circuit or sets, chosen when it is created and changeable in the builder (see the amendment below), and is made of <strong>parts</strong>: an optional warm-up, one work part and an optional cool down.</p><p>The five type modules had converged on one rule: a time or a hold runs a timer, and reps, a distance or an open target wait for a tap. The type only decided which exercises the builder offered and a label, while people kept mixing a run warm-up with squats or a walk cool down with stretches. What they actually choose is how the workout is built: rounds through several exercises, each done once per round, or one exercise at a time with all its sets. That is the format. Warming up and cooling down are sections of the workout rather than tags on single steps, so they became parts.</p><p>The engine reads the completion rule from the target, compiles the parts in order, and tells each phase which part it belongs to. <code>WorkoutTypeModule</code>, its registry and the strength, conditioning, rehab and mobility modules are gone; <code>types/treadmill</code> stays for the treadmill&#39;s own logic (recording, summary, step estimate). What the type used to drive elsewhere comes from the format or from the exercises: the Live picker offers workouts with a walk or a run (<a href="./0018-live-plays-mixed-workouts-with-the-players-steps">ADR 0018</a>), and the FIT sport follows the format and the exercises.</p><p>Saved workouts were not converted when this landed: the app was only on its author&#39;s phones, so the migration removes them and their planned days, and past sessions stay in the history without a format.</p><h2 id="amendment-the-format-can-be-changed" tabindex="-1">Amendment: the format can be changed <a class="header-anchor" href="#amendment-the-format-can-be-changed" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Amendment: the format can be changed&quot;">​</a></h2><p>The format was first chosen once and kept. Someone who wants the same exercises done differently had to rebuild the workout, so the builder now lets them switch it (Circuit ⇄ Sets) from the format line under the name. The format is still chosen first when a workout is created, now in a sheet over the page that asked, with no Continue: a tap on Circuit or Sets opens the builder with that format.</p><p>The switch is about the order the exercises are done in, so it asks. <strong>Follow the new format</strong> reorders them the way the new format does them (A A A B B B ⇄ A B, A B, A B); <strong>Keep the order</strong> rewrites only the steps to fit the format&#39;s one rule (an exercise step of a circuit holds one set and the repeat around it does the rounds). The conversion is one pure function in shared Kotlin (<code>switchedTo</code> in <code>FormatSwitch.kt</code>) and its result always passes <code>Workout.problems()</code>; the warm-up and the cool down always keep the order. It applies to the draft; saving stores a new version like any edit, and past sessions keep pointing at the version they followed. No migration: each version already stores its own format (<code>workout_version.format</code>).</p><p>Not changed: <code>SETS_IN_CIRCUIT</code> stays, so the format is still more than a presentation.</p><h2 id="amendment-one-work-part" tabindex="-1">Amendment: one work part <a class="header-anchor" href="#amendment-one-work-part" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Amendment: one work part&quot;">​</a></h2><p>A workout has exactly one work part. A second work part added only a labelled chunk with its own subtotal; in a circuit, separate repeats do that job, and in sets each exercise carries its own sets. The builder mockups (design/builder-tidy) show it. <code>Workout.problems()</code> reports <code>SEVERAL_WORK_PARTS</code> for more than one, so such a workout cannot be built, imported or edited; no migration was written while the app is in test.</p>',12)])])}const u=t(s,[["render",n]]);export{m as __pageData,u as default};
